WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:07.399
Whether animated, on film, or on TV, Gotham's
resident murdering maniac The Joker is always

00:00:07.399 --> 00:00:08.399
memorable.

00:00:08.399 --> 00:00:13.050
For every actor who’s given his unnerving
cackle to the Joker, there are plenty of notable

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:16.139
actors who just narrowly missed the part.

00:00:16.139 --> 00:00:19.660
Here are some actors who almost played the
Joker.

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:20.820
José Ferrer

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:26.329
Now you might not be familiar with José Ferrer's
acting résumé, but the Academy Award winner

00:00:26.329 --> 00:00:33.260
was a big deal when producers were casting
the original 1966 Batman TV series.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:38.940
According to producer William Dozier’s original
production notes, Ferrer was reportedly up

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:43.149
for the role of the Joker, but the actor had
scheduling issues.

00:00:43.149 --> 00:00:49.550
"We also tried one other for The Joker, and
I'm glad we didn't get him, because I think

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:51.949
Cesar Romero is just magnificent."

00:00:51.949 --> 00:00:58.579
Cesar Romero eventually edged Ferrer out...even
though Romero refused to shave his trademark

00:00:58.579 --> 00:00:59.800
mustache.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:06.110
Despite Ferrer missing the part, his son Miguel
went on to voice a slew of famous DC Comics

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:10.650
characters, including Martian Manhunter, Weather
Wizard, and Aquaman.

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:16.409
"Surface dwellers, they're about to cause
the death of millions, I have to stop them."

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:17.409
Frank Sinatra

00:01:17.409 --> 00:01:23.500
Adam West's campy Batman attracted some huge
Hollywood stars as important villains, including

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:27.640
Eartha Kitt, Burgess Meredith, and Frank Gorshin.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:33.159
According to Burt Ward’s memoir, Boy Wonder:
My Life in Tights, Frank Sinatra wanted in

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:39.259
on the Joker action, but arrived after Cesar
Romero had already signed on, upsetting the

00:01:39.259 --> 00:01:40.280
hopeful Sinatra.

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:49.770
(Sinatra singing sad clown song, poor guy)

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:55.520
While a crooning Joker certainly would have
made the clown's elaborate cruelties even

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:01.549
weirder, Sinatra posthumously scored his Joker
connection in Rocksteady's Batman: Arkham

00:02:01.549 --> 00:02:07.250
Knight game, where his rendition of "I've
Got You Under My Skin" plays a vital role

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:08.790
in a Joker-related scene.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:14.800
Tim Curry

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:20.769
No stranger to playing monsters and creepy
clowns, Tim Curry missed out on the role of

00:02:20.769 --> 00:02:23.480
the Joker in two separate productions.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:29.830
Curry was among many familiar actors who were
on Tim Burton's shortlist for the iconic 1989

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:35.440
Batman, but the eternally-creepy Jack Nicholson
ultimately won the role.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:42.489
Curry then went on to record multiple episodes
of Batman: The Animated Series as the Joker...before

00:02:42.489 --> 00:02:45.019
being replaced by Mark Hamill.

00:02:45.019 --> 00:02:52.159
Reportedly, Curry's Joker was a bit too terrifying
for the kiddie demographic, and it also strained

00:02:52.159 --> 00:02:53.159
Curry’s voice.

00:02:53.159 --> 00:02:57.370
He did, however, lend his laugh to a robot
clown later in the series.

00:02:57.370 --> 00:02:58.370
Close enough.

00:02:58.370 --> 00:02:59.709
"Ah ha ha ha!

00:02:59.709 --> 00:03:01.280
Ah ha ha ha!

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:02.849
Ah ha ha ha!"

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:03.849
Robin Williams

00:03:03.849 --> 00:03:08.560
One of the greatest Batman tragedies is the
fact that Robin Williams never had a chance

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:09.620
to play the Joker.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:15.550
As a true comic nerd, Williams revealed in
a 2010 interview with Empire Magazine that

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:20.760
he was thrilled when he was offered the villainous
role in Burton’s Batman after Nicholson

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.500
first declined the part.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:24.739
Until Nicholson changed his mind.

00:03:24.739 --> 00:03:26.900
The two seemed to remain cordial, however.

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:31.370
"When I won my Academy Award he was there,
he won, this was his 3rd Academy Award.

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:34.620
We're standing backstage cause they're just
about to take you to the press conference,

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:38.000
and he goes, 'Ya know Rob-o now I have one
for every decade.'

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:41.620
You bastard!"

00:03:41.629 --> 00:03:47.519
Williams had a second chance at the franchise
as the Riddler in Batman Forever, but again,

00:03:47.519 --> 00:03:51.490
the role was yanked out from under him in
favor of Jim Carrey.

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:56.819
According to a 2006 IGN interview, Williams
also tried to get into Christopher Nolan's

00:03:56.819 --> 00:04:00.159
trilogy, but was passed over for Heath Ledger.

00:04:00.159 --> 00:04:03.209
No actor had ever wanted it so badly.

00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:04.659
John Lithgow

00:04:04.659 --> 00:04:09.909
Also on the shortlist to play Tim Burton's
Joker, John Lithgow reportedly declined the

00:04:09.909 --> 00:04:15.030
role, stating that the character didn't seem
appropriate for his particular set of skills.

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:18.120
"Don't care whooo whooo whooo!"

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:25.250
Many years later, Lithgow would play an incredible,
viciously memorable serial killer on Dexter,

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:29.790
proving that the depths of his depiction of
evil would have been excellent against Michael

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:31.070
Keaton's Batman.

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:37.200
Instead, Lithgow wasted his time in 1989 on
a made-for-TV movie.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:38.430
Willem Dafoe

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:43.000
When you kinda look like the Joker before
you even sit in the makeup chair, you're an

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:44.980
easy casting decision.

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:50.470
Willem Dafoe was another actor who was briefly
considered for the 1989 film role, simply

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:53.280
because he looked the part naturally.

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:59.750
He later landed the role of the Green Goblin
in 2002’s Spider-Man, another cackling supervillain

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:02.960
who is pretty much Marvel's answer to the
Joker anyhow.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:05.480
"Heh heh heh heh"

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:06.860
Sam Rockwell

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:12.480
Peek through Sam Rockwell's acting history
and you'll see that he can do just about anything.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:19.360
He's rarely failed a role, even as the nameless
punk ringleader in Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles.

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:24.360
Rockwell was considered by Christopher Nolan
for The Dark Knight, but producers ultimately

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:29.660
went with Heath Ledger instead, leaving this
caliber of performance behind.

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:34.650
"Oh my holy crap, surveillance does, I hate
those."

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:36.170
Adrien Brody

00:05:36.170 --> 00:05:40.710
Another Dark Knight contender, Adrien Brody
has publicly expressed an interest in playing

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:43.260
the Joker, according to MoviePilot.com.

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:49.900
Rumors arose regarding Brody making a Joker
cameo in Batman versus Superman: Dawn of Justice,

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:55.140
but Jared Leto ultimately took over in the
role in DC's Extended Universe.

00:05:55.140 --> 00:06:01.270
Brody stated at Cannes in 2014 that no one
ever approaches him for comic book roles,

00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:06.270
but there's truly no better role to fill with
completely offbeat casting.

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:07.850
Ryan Gosling

00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:13.150
According to The Wrap, Suicide Squad's director
David Ayer was aiming for Ryan Gosling to

00:06:13.150 --> 00:06:17.900
play the Clown Prince of Crime, but got Jared
Leto instead.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:23.050
Gosling's pretty-boy Joker would have been
a bizarre contrast to the twisted Jokers of

00:06:23.050 --> 00:06:28.220
years past, but it's hard to get the ever-changing
Joker totally wrong.

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:33.140
It's the Joker's paradox: the more wrong you
make him, the better he gets.

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:35.380
That laugh is on point, though.

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:37.780
"Ah ha ha ha ha haaaa!"

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:38.780
Thanks for watching!

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:42.370
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:46.000
And leave us a comment to let us know which
Joker you wish you could've seen...

